                    - I thought the school was so clean and organized. Classrooms, student lounge and restroom were kept clean all the time. Computers, vending machine, refrigerator, microwave, and cooler were provided for all students in the student lounge, so students were able to enjoy their break time there. In addition, MLI is located in a perfect place with sightseeing spots and many kinds of restaurants. As a student I was able to go sightseeing after school and learn about different cultures at various restaurants.
                    
                    What do you like to do in your free time?
                    - There are interesting activities at MLI; Christmas party, Thanksgiving party, Valentine\'s day and Graduation party for example. At the Halloween party, students, teachers and staff wore high quality costumes to just have fun. There is Santa Monica Beach near MLI, so I often go there on weekends. The weather is so nice in LA, so I take a walk by the beach and get sunburned to feel “California”.
                    
                    What was difficult in LA?
                    - I was confused and had a hard time getting used to going to supermarkets and taking buses and the subway because they were totally different from Japan. What made me feel disappointed was the fact that I could not speak English fluently when I needed someone’s help. That made me not want to go out sometimes. It did not happen to me, but some students even felt home-sick and missed Japanese food a lot.
                    
                    Any special episode while you were in LA?
                    - The culture of lady first, period. I was so impressed that men open the door for women every time when getting in a car. What I was impressed the most was when a waiter at a restaurant was going to refill water for a man, he realized there was an empty glass for a lady and he stopped serving the man and started serving the lady first. It would never happen in Japan. As women, I was so impressed, but I do not know how men would think.
                    
                    How is your English improved after coming to LA?
                    - While I was in LA, a friend of mine came to see me. When we went shopping and sightseeing together, I was translating everything to her and she said she was proud of me because of it. This was the moment I finally felt my English had been improved since I did not have confidence about it. Her words still mean a lot to me.
                    
                    What did you earn other than English?
                    - Friends. I tried to make friendships with foreigners so that I could speak English daily. I was able to make a lot of close friends in the end. None of us could speak English fluently, but we spent time together going shopping, having lunches and partying, and we just had a lot of fun. They mean a lot to me, and I cannot imagine my life without them now.'
                ],
